WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.256
Mia, our guitar has a mystery.

00:00:02.748 --> 00:00:05.105
Pluck the string gently
and we hear a note.

00:00:05.416 --> 00:00:07.977
Dampen its vibration
and the note dies away.

00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:09.658
Where did the sound come from?

00:00:10.447 --> 00:00:11.978
The string was vibrating.

00:00:12.402 --> 00:00:14.962
It was moving backwards and
forwards rather than going

00:00:14.963 --> 00:00:16.197
on a trip across the room.

00:00:17.012 --> 00:00:17.791
Exactly.

00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:20.271
Vibrating sources can produce sound.

00:00:20.509 --> 00:00:24.848
A drum skin, a loudspeaker cone, and
your vocal folds are other examples.

00:00:25.413 --> 00:00:28.086
The motion may be too small
or fast to see clearly.

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:31.991
So a louder sound means the
vibration must be faster?

00:00:32.807 --> 00:00:33.688
Hold that thought.

00:00:34.024 --> 00:00:36.322
You have just set our first exam trap.

00:00:36.655 --> 00:00:40.459
Faster vibration and bigger
vibration affect different features.

00:00:40.769 --> 00:00:42.555
We will rescue that answer shortly.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.932
First, the vibrating source
disturbs the material around it.

00:00:47.152 --> 00:00:49.711
The disturbance passes
from one region to another.

00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:54.227
That traveling disturbance is a wave
transferring energy through a medium.

00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:59.667
Medium means the substance carrying
the sound: air, water, or a solid,

00:00:59.951 --> 00:01:01.739
not a middle-sized loudspeaker.

00:01:02.494 --> 00:01:03.092
Correct.

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.494
Sound can travel through gases,
liquids, and solids, but it cannot

00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:11.079
travel through an ideal vacuum
because there is no material there

00:01:11.096 --> 00:01:12.942
to carry the mechanical disturbance.

00:01:13.713 --> 00:01:18.643
Then a spaceship explosion heard
through empty space is cinematic sound

00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:21.213
design, not a physics demonstration.

00:01:21.606 --> 00:01:25.030
Imagine pushing and pulling
one end of a stretched spring.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.112
Squashed and spread out
regions travel along it.

00:01:28.506 --> 00:01:31.330
Does each coil travel all
the way to the other end?

00:01:32.191 --> 00:01:32.534
No.

00:01:32.911 --> 00:01:36.398
A marked coil moves back and
forth near its original position

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:37.832
while the pattern travels.

00:01:38.191 --> 00:01:40.789
The material and the
disturbance do different things.

00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:41.979
Good.

00:01:42.255 --> 00:01:43.922
Sound in air is longitudinal.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:47.460
Local particle motion is parallel
to the direction of wave travel.

00:01:47.749 --> 00:01:50.191
Compressions have particles
more crowded together.

00:01:50.485 --> 00:01:52.547
Rarefactions have them more spread out.

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:56.889
So air particles do not have to
travel from your mouth into my

00:01:56.889 --> 00:01:58.744
ear to deliver every sentence.

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:00.122
Exactly.

00:02:00.457 --> 00:02:05.254
Ordinary sound involves small oscillations
superimposed on particles' other motion.

00:02:05.584 --> 00:02:09.917
The wave carries energy, not a stream
of air delivering the same particles

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:11.596
all the way from speaker to listener.

00:02:12.443 --> 00:02:13.343
A rope wave

00:02:13.366 --> 00:02:14.146
is different.

00:02:14.548 --> 00:02:14.638
A

00:02:14.638 --> 00:02:15.137
section of

00:02:15.137 --> 00:02:18.933
rope can move up and down while
the disturbance travels sideways.

00:02:19.729 --> 00:02:23.999
That is a transverse wave,
vibration perpendicular to travel.

00:02:24.356 --> 00:02:27.987
Electromagnetic waves are transverse
too, but their oscillations are

00:02:27.987 --> 00:02:30.278
fields, not material particles.

00:02:30.632 --> 00:02:32.085
They can travel through a vacuum.

00:02:32.854 --> 00:02:34.262
What about water ripples?

00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:36.400
They look like traveling humps.

00:02:37.164 --> 00:02:40.837
A transverse model is useful at
school, but real surface water

00:02:40.837 --> 00:02:44.462
particles often follow roughly
circular or elliptical paths.

00:02:44.847 --> 00:02:48.326
Do not assume every water
particle moves purely up and down.

00:02:49.124 --> 00:02:52.829
In a bell jar, removing air makes
the ringing fainter, even though

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:54.385
we can still see the bell moving.

00:02:55.109 --> 00:02:55.682
Yes.

00:02:55.873 --> 00:02:57.662
That supports the need for a medium.

00:02:58.191 --> 00:03:02.733
Real demonstrations may retain some sound
through supports or residual air, so

00:03:02.734 --> 00:03:05.140
perfect silence is not essential evidence.

00:03:05.543 --> 00:03:08.540
Light still reaches our eyes
through the evacuated space.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:09.857
Back to your trap.

00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:13.797
Frequency means how many complete
vibrations occur each second.

00:03:14.081 --> 00:03:15.506
Its unit is hertz.

00:03:15.882 --> 00:03:18.949
What changes when the frequency
of a simple tone increases?

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.083
Its pitch becomes higher.

00:03:21.527 --> 00:03:26.152
One hundred hertz means one hundred
complete vibrations each second.

00:03:26.965 --> 00:03:27.513
Correct.

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:32.582
Amplitude means the maximum displacement
from the rest position, not the entire

00:03:32.582 --> 00:03:34.502
distance from one extreme to the other.

00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:39.473
Larger amplitude generally corresponds
to greater sound intensity and

00:03:39.473 --> 00:03:41.011
greater perceived loudness.

00:03:41.762 --> 00:03:46.453
But loudness is how we perceive sound, so
our ears and the frequency matter as well?

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.017
Exactly.

00:03:48.362 --> 00:03:51.618
The school comparison works best
for otherwise comparable sounds.

00:03:52.016 --> 00:03:54.541
Pitch and loudness are
not interchangeable.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:58.219
A high note can be quiet,
and a low note can be loud.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:03.138
I can imagine a quiet high
whistle, a loud high whistle, a

00:04:03.138 --> 00:04:05.970
quiet low hum, and a loud low hum.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:08.616
Four combinations, not just two.

00:04:09.388 --> 00:04:09.796
Good.

00:04:10.315 --> 00:04:14.647
Plucking the same guitar string harder
mainly increases its initial amplitude.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:19.177
Shortening or tightening an otherwise
similar string raises its frequency.

00:04:19.681 --> 00:04:22.502
String thickness and mass
per length also matter.

00:04:23.281 --> 00:04:27.587
Then hitting the same drum harder does
not automatically make its note higher.

00:04:27.998 --> 00:04:31.997
I describe the main change
as amplitude and loudness.

00:04:32.752 --> 00:04:33.530
Exactly.

00:04:33.832 --> 00:04:38.118
If you write frequency when the question
asks why a trace is louder, our imaginary

00:04:38.119 --> 00:04:39.905
Mark may leave the concert early.
